WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.980
Settle into a seated or resting position that supports both ease and alertness.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.925
This practice explores a center and a wider field.

3
00:00:14.625 --> 00:00:19.755
Nothing needs to disappear for attention to become steady.

4
00:00:25.602 --> 00:00:28.042
Begin with the body as a whole.

5
00:00:29.742 --> 00:00:33.987
Feel its weight, shape, and contact with support.

6
00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:41.702
Let sounds and light be present without deciding which one matters most.

7
00:00:53.884 --> 00:01:02.089
Choose one simple center for attention: breath, one hand, or contact beneath the feet.

8
00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:11.169
Let it be clear enough to find again and neutral enough to stay with gently.

9
00:01:23.351 --> 00:01:25.871
Follow the center for three natural breaths.

10
00:01:27.571 --> 00:01:33.121
Notice the details that change and the details that seem steady.

11
00:01:34.821 --> 00:01:38.966
There is no need to narrow the mind around them.

12
00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.300
Now include the nearest surrounding sensation.

13
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.005
If breath is central, include the chest or belly.

14
00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:14.125
If a hand is central, include the arm and the space around it.

15
00:02:28.743 --> 00:02:33.953
Keep the center recognizable while the field becomes wider.

16
00:02:35.653 --> 00:02:42.793
Think of a lamp lighting one object clearly without making the rest of the room dark.

17
00:02:59.848 --> 00:03:01.128
Include sounds.

18
00:03:02.828 --> 00:03:08.623
Let the closest and farthest sounds arrive within awareness.

19
00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:16.463
The center remains available, and hearing does not have to compete with it.

20
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:39.087
Notice when attention jumps entirely toward a sound or thought.

21
00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:45.812
Return to the center, then gently reopen the wider field.

22
00:03:47.512 --> 00:03:50.592
This movement can happen many times.

23
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:22.568
Include the whole body now: face, shoulders, torso, arms, legs, and contact with support.

24
00:04:24.268 --> 00:04:30.928
Keep one small place in clearer focus while the rest is held more softly.

25
00:04:50.418 --> 00:04:56.898
Perhaps effort appears as staring inward, holding the breath, or tensing the forehead.

26
00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:00.078
Let the gaze soften.

27
00:05:01.778 --> 00:05:03.378
Let breathing continue.

28
00:05:05.078 --> 00:05:10.473
Spacious attention is receptive, not rigid.

29
00:05:27.528 --> 00:05:32.383
For a few moments, alternate deliberately.

30
00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:36.163
Narrow toward the chosen center.

31
00:05:37.863 --> 00:05:40.423
Then widen to body and room.

32
00:05:42.123 --> 00:05:43.443
Narrow.

33
00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:46.783
Then widen.

34
00:05:48.483 --> 00:05:53.543
Learn the felt difference without declaring one better.

35
00:06:17.906 --> 00:06:24.391
Now stop alternating and allow both scales at once.

36
00:06:26.091 --> 00:06:28.851
The center can be precise.

37
00:06:30.551 --> 00:06:32.191
The field can be broad.

38
00:06:33.891 --> 00:06:38.711
Attention does not have to choose only one size.

39
00:06:58.202 --> 00:07:00.242
A thought may arise in the field.

40
00:07:01.942 --> 00:07:06.502
Notice its presence without entering the whole story.

41
00:07:08.202 --> 00:07:16.492
Let it be one event among breath, pressure, sound, temperature, and light.

42
00:07:33.546 --> 00:07:39.691
If an emotion is present, include only as much as feels manageable.

43
00:07:41.391 --> 00:07:47.021
Keep contact with the supporting surface and with sounds outside the body.

44
00:07:48.721 --> 00:07:53.516
You may return to a smaller neutral center whenever needed.

45
00:08:13.007 --> 00:08:18.512
Notice the edges of the visual field through closed or open eyes.

46
00:08:20.212 --> 00:08:28.152
Without moving toward anything, sense that experience extends beyond the point you are focusing on.

47
00:08:45.207 --> 00:08:58.912
Bring to mind one ordinary task that benefits from this quality: reading, listening, writing, cooking, or speaking with someone.

48
00:09:00.612 --> 00:09:06.437
Imagine keeping the task central while still noticing body and surroundings.

49
00:09:23.491 --> 00:09:25.771
Return to immediate sensation.

50
00:09:27.471 --> 00:09:28.871
Center and field.

51
00:09:30.571 --> 00:09:32.811
Detail and context.

52
00:09:34.511 --> 00:09:36.631
Neither has to be held perfectly.

53
00:09:38.331 --> 00:09:45.461
Each time the mind collapses into one thing, gently make room again.

54
00:10:07.388 --> 00:10:09.468
Let the center become less emphasized.

55
00:10:11.168 --> 00:10:24.458
Rest with the whole field for several breaths: sounds, body, space, and the changing movement of attention itself.

56
00:10:46.385 --> 00:10:52.885
Choose the center once more and feel how easily it can be found after widening.

57
00:10:54.585 --> 00:10:56.585
Clear focus has not been lost.

58
00:10:58.285 --> 00:11:00.965
It now lives within more context.

59
00:11:15.583 --> 00:11:23.043
Begin to close the practice by feeling both feet or the full area of support.

60
00:11:24.743 --> 00:11:26.463
Notice the room clearly.

61
00:11:28.163 --> 00:11:32.478
Let your eyes open more fully if they were closed.

62
00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.780
Move in a way that feels useful.

63
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.860
You practiced keeping one thing important without pretending it was the only thing present.

64
00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.000
Carry that balance into one next task.
